# Herdr

Herdr -> terminals into workspaces, tabs, panes. Recognizes coding agents in panes. Session via `herdr` CLI.

Verify inside Herdr-managed pane before any control command:

```bash
test "${HERDR_ENV:-}" = 1
```

Fail -> say not inside Herdr, stop. Never inspect/control focused session from outside Herdr.

Pass -> `herdr` in `PATH` talks to current session: inspect neighbor work, create layout, start agents/commands, read output, wait for state.

## Learn the current CLI

Installed binary = syntax authority. Start:

```bash
herdr --help
```

Print group by running it without subcommand:

```bash
herdr agent
herdr pane
herdr workspace
herdr tab
herdr worktree
herdr terminal
herdr notification
herdr integration
herdr session
herdr machine
```

Never run bare `herdr` for discovery; launches/attaches TUI. Never probe mutating nested command by omitting args; `herdr workspace create` valid with defaults, executes.

Most control commands -> JSON. Read identifiers/state from responses; never predict.

## Understand layout, panes, and agents

Pick primitive matching job:

- Workspace/tab/pane topology -> terminal locations.
- Pane commands -> raw terminals, shells, tests, servers, input, output.
- Agent commands -> recognized agent occupying pane.

Pane exists with/without agent. `agent start` needs existing available shell pane; never creates/splits/moves layout. Ordinary processes -> pane commands. Identity validation or `idle`/`working`/`blocked`/`done`/`unknown` state -> agent commands.

Agent commands take unique live agent name or hosting pane ID; not terminal IDs, not bare kind labels. Names `[a-z][a-z0-9_-]{0,31}`, unique among live agents, follow pane occupant, cleared on exit/release/replacement.

`idle`/`done` = ready for input; CLI/API splits via server seen state (explicit focus marks seen, reads do not). TUI clients track completions independently; Done badge can differ from CLI/other client. `blocked` = recognized approval/question UI. `unknown` = agent present, unclassifiable; no completion proof.

## Use IDs and caller context

Public IDs = opaque stable handles: workspace `w1`, tab `w1:t1`, pane `w1:p1`.

Closed tab/pane IDs not reused. Moved pane -> new workspace-qualified ID. After `pane move` use `.result.move_result.pane.pane_id` or live agent name; old ID `.result.move_result.previous_pane_id` resolves only for moved process's inherited caller context, not a general target.

Herdr injects caller context into each managed pane:

```bash
printf '%s\n' "$HERDR_WORKSPACE_ID" "$HERDR_TAB_ID" "$HERDR_PANE_ID"
```

Prefer `--current` when targeting calling pane. Omitted `pane split` target = calling pane if `HERDR_PANE_ID` set, else focused pane. Other commands may use UI-focused pane (user/another client).

Discover live state:

```bash
herdr workspace list
herdr tab list --workspace "$HERDR_WORKSPACE_ID"
herdr pane current --current
herdr pane list --workspace "$HERDR_WORKSPACE_ID"
herdr agent list
```

Creation responses expose next IDs: `workspace create` -> `.result.workspace`/`.result.tab`/`.result.root_pane`; `tab create` -> `.result.tab`/`.result.root_pane`; `pane split` -> `.result.pane`.

IDs/agent names scoped to one server: two saved SSH machines can share `w1:p1` or agent `reviewer`. TUI machine selection does not retarget your pane's commands; without `--machine`, inherited session/socket used.

Saved SSH machine -> same global prefix for discovery + every later command:

```bash
herdr --machine <label-or-id> agent list
herdr --machine <label-or-id> pane list
herdr --machine <label-or-id> agent prompt <remote-agent-name> "Reply with your current status." --wait --timeout 120000
```

Selector = enabled saved profile ID or unique case-sensitive label, not arbitrary SSH hostname. Commands use profile's remote session, no open TUI. Never combine `--machine` with `--session`/`--remote`. Discover IDs on that machine; inherited local IDs and `--current` do not identify remote panes.

Need machine API forwarding both sides; remote server already running + API-compatible. Forwarding never installs/starts/restarts server, never falls back to Local. Local config, session management, install commands, interactive attachment not forwarded. Remote worktree paths absolute, `~`, or `~/...`; plugin link paths absolute. Connection failure does not prove mutation not applied; inspect remote state before retry.

`machine list` = saved profiles, not cross-machine pane inventory (`--json` for scripts). Add/remove/enable/disable only on user request. Remove disconnects client, leaves remote sessions. Interactive `machine add` discovers running remote sessions, may ask user to pick; non-interactive uses remote default session unless `--remote-session`. Diagnose: `herdr machine status [<label-or-id>] --json`. `machine reconnect` needs user SSH auth; never run for them. Setup asks before stopping incompatible server, defaults No; no replacement without user consent. Experimental handoff not in `machine add`.

## Start and coordinate an agent

Default -> sibling pane, current tab, current working directory. No workspace/tab/worktree/different cwd unless user requests.

Honor user direction; else inspect caller pane:

```bash
herdr pane layout --pane "$HERDR_PANE_ID"
```

Wide pane -> split right; narrow/tall -> split down. Avoid repeated same-direction splits (unusably narrow columns/short rows). Keep user focus in calling pane; preserve caller cwd:

```bash
herdr pane split --current --direction right --cwd "$PWD" --no-focus
```

Replace `right` with `down` when appropriate. New pane ID -> `.result.pane.pane_id`.

Available shell pane must be at interactive prompt, shell foreground, no foreground command/editor/agent. Start supported agent there with useful unique name:

```bash
herdr agent start reviewer --kind codex --pane <returned-pane-id>
```

Use user-requested kind. `herdr agent` -> kind list + options. Native agent args only after `--`:

```bash
herdr agent start reviewer --kind codex --pane <returned-pane-id> -- <agent-args...>
```

`agent start` returns only after Herdr detects expected agent in same pane and considers it ready for interactive input. Startup block -> returns `agent_not_ready` immediately; name still usable for `agent read`/`agent send-keys`. Wait until idle before prompting. Startup default timeout 30s.

Submit work:

```bash
herdr agent prompt reviewer "Review the current diff and report only actionable findings." --wait --timeout 120000
```

`agent prompt` honors pane live bracketed-paste mode; sends text + encoded Enter as one ordered submission. Success = both written; does not prove turn started. Codex on Windows -> paste boundary before Enter; submission independent of prompt size. Rejects agent at approval/question dialog with `agent_blocked` before sending. Inspect blocked UI; ask user before answering. Normal work: `--wait` = first settled `idle`/`done`/`blocked`; do not repeat with `--until`.

`--wait`: prompt from non-working state must produce observed `working`/`blocked`; Herdr waits up to 5s after submission, unrelated `idle`/`done`/session changes do not satisfy. Returns `agent_prompt_stalled` (no activity) or `timeout` (caller timeout first; includes submission time). No timeout -> indefinite settled-state wait after activity. Tracks lifecycle, not turn; active-turn completion may satisfy if already working.

`--until` only for state-specific workflow, e.g. wait for running agent to request input:

```bash
herdr agent wait reviewer --until blocked --timeout 120000
```

Without `--until`, standalone `agent wait` = same settled-state defaults as `agent prompt --wait`.

Logical keys for interactive agent UI:

```bash
herdr agent send-keys reviewer esc
herdr agent send-keys reviewer ctrl+c
```

Herdr validates all keys before writing bytes. Read result via resolved agent:

```bash
herdr agent get reviewer
herdr agent read reviewer --source recent-unwrapped --lines 120
```

Wait fails/returns `blocked` -> inspect `agent get` + `agent read` before deciding input. Timeout/stalled does not prove prompt never delivered; do not blindly resubmit. Pane surface only for intentional raw terminal control.

## Run an ordinary command in another pane

Sibling pane, same geometry rule, caller cwd preserved, user focus unchanged:

```bash
herdr pane split --current --direction right --cwd "$PWD" --no-focus
```

New pane ID -> `.result.pane.pane_id`; then run + inspect:

```bash
herdr pane run <returned-pane-id> "just test"
herdr pane wait-output <returned-pane-id> --match "test result" --timeout 120000
herdr pane read <returned-pane-id> --source recent-unwrapped --lines 120
```

`pane run` atomically sends command text + Enter. `pane wait-output` searches selected snapshot immediately; existing output can match. `--match <text>` literal substring; `--regex <pattern>` Rust regex. Omit `--timeout` -> indefinite wait.

Read source by task:

- `visible`: current rendered viewport.
- `recent`: recent rendered output, soft wraps included.
- `recent-unwrapped`: recent output, soft wraps joined; prefer for logs/transcripts.
- `detection`: plain-text bottom-buffer snapshot for agent detection.

`--format ansi` when colors/styling are evidence; else text.

`--lines` -> more rows from pane available screen + host scrollback. Alternate-screen rows do not enter ordinary host scrollback. Supported idle agents: Herdr collects application-owned history and restores viewport afterward; not every application/response recoverable.

Larger recent read still misses completed response -> ask agent to write Markdown in temporary dir, reply only file path, read file same machine. Fallback only; never request file output in initial prompt.

## Safety and coordination rules

- `--no-focus` for background work unless user asked context switch.
- `--current`, explicit pane ID, or unique agent name; never rely on another client's focused pane.
- Parse IDs from JSON responses; never derive from sidebar order/examples.
- Do not close workspaces/tabs/panes/sessions you did not create unless user explicitly asked. `workspace close --group` closes primary + linked worktree workspaces; never add merely to bypass `workspace_group_close_required`.
- `--trust-repository` only after user verified repository. Per-request Git trust; not routine retry for failed worktree command.
- Client/server versions can differ after update. Check `herdr status` before relying on new server features. Missing method does not permit stop/upgrade.
- Never run `herdr server stop` from active session unless user explicitly intends to stop server + pane processes.
- Never kill main Herdr process. Named test sessions for isolated-server experiments.
- CLI server errors -> JSON on stderr, exit status 1. CLI syntax errors -> exit status 2.
